{"builder":"Goal: when a config file has malformed JSON, the error must name the file path and the line and column of the mistake. The app must still exit 1 with a `bad config:` line, and valid configs must load exactly as before.\n\nCurrent code: `src/config.js` `loadConfig(path)` returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`. `src/app.js` catches any error, writes `bad config: ${error.message}` to stderr and exits 1. `test/config.test.js` has two tests run by `npm test` (`node --test`). One of them asserts that bad JSON throws a `SyntaxError`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In `src/config.js`, read the file as now. Wrap only the `JSON.parse` call in try/catch. On a parse error, throw a new error (keeping it a `SyntaxError` is simplest, since the existing test expects one). Its `message` must contain the path exactly as passed in (do not resolve it to an absolute path), the line number, the column number and the original parse message. A suggested format is `<path>:<line>:<column>: <original message>`, or `<path> line <L> column <C>: ...`. Set `cause` to the original error.\n2. Work out the line and column yourself; do not depend only on Node's wording. Take the character position from the original message (`/position (\\d+)/`). Count the newlines in the text before that position to get the line, and the characters since the last newline to get the column, both 1-based. If Node's message already gives `(line N column M)`, you may use those numbers, but fall back to computing them from the position. If neither is available (for example, unexpected end of input with no position), use the end of the text. Check the result: for the file `{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n`, Node v22 reports position 20, and that must give line 3, column 3.\n3. Do not catch errors from `readFileSync`. A missing file must still throw and produce an exit-1 `bad config:` failure. Never return `{}` or defaults for a malformed file. Do not add JSON5 or comment support, or any validation of config values.\n4. `src/app.js` should need no change, because it already prints `bad config: ${error.message}` and exits 1. Make sure nothing goes to stdout on failure.\n5. Add tests to `test/config.test.js`:\n   - The four-line file above makes `loadConfig` throw an error whose message includes the temp file path, `3` as the line and `3` as the column.\n   - `{port: 9000` throws, and the message includes the path.\n   - Keep the existing tests passing: valid `{\"port\": 9000}` deep-equals `{ port: 9000 }`.\n   - Optionally, spawn `node src/app.js <file>` with `child_process` and assert exit 1, empty stdout, and stderr containing `bad config:` and the path.\n   The path tests must fail against the current `src/config.js`.\n6. Optionally, update the README to say the `bad config:` message now includes the file path and location.\n7. Run `npm test` and the manual checks: `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json` with the four-line file must exit 1 with empty stdout and stderr naming the path, line 3 and column 3. `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json` with `{\"port\": 9000}` must exit 0 and print `listening on 9000`. Then open one pull request.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head under Node v22 (check with `node --version`), from the repository root. Each check runs commands; reading the diff alone is not enough.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json >$d/out 2>$d/err; echo \"exit $?\"; wc -c < $d/out; cat $d/out`\nPASS: the exit code is 1 and `$d/out` is empty (0 bytes).\nFAIL: any other exit code, or any stdout output, including a `listening on` line.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.\"\nRun: using the same `$d` as Check 1, `cat $d/err; grep -F 'bad config:' $d/err; grep -F \"$d/c.json\" $d/err`.\nPASS: stderr contains `bad config:` and the exact string `$d/c.json`, and it shows line 3 and column 3 of the mistake, in a form such as `line 3 column 3`, `3:3` or `line 3, column 3`. A file-location format such as `<path>:3:3` also counts.\nFAIL: any of these is missing, the path is shown in another form (for example resolved differently or shortened), or the line or column shown is not 3.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > $d/b.json; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"`\nPASS: it prints `OK`.\nFAIL: it prints `RETURNED ...` or `NO PATH: ...`, or it crashes in some other way.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\"port\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; console.log(JSON.stringify(loadConfig('$d/ok.json')))\"`\nPASS: stdout shows `listening on 9000`, the exit code is 0, and the second command prints `{\"port\":9000}`.\nFAIL: any of these differs.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\"\nRun:\n1. `npm test` at the PR head.\n2. Find the base commit with `git merge-base HEAD <the PR's target ref>`. Keep the PR's `test/` files, check out the base's `src/` with `git checkout <base> -- src`, and run `npm test` again.\n3. Restore with `git checkout HEAD -- src`.\nPASS: step 1 passes with no failures, and in step 2 at least one test fails that asserts the malformed-JSON error message includes the file path.\nFAIL: step 1 has any failure; no test asserts the path in the error; or every such test still passes against the base `src/`.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS/FAIL):\n- Read `src/config.js` to see whether the line and column are computed from the parse position, rather than taken only from Node's message text. Relying only on Node's message is fragile across Node versions, so mention it if so.\n- Confirm that a missing config file still gives an exit-1 `bad config:` failure (`node src/app.js /nonexistent.json`).\n- Confirm that `loadConfig` never returns `{}` or defaults for malformed input."}